@h1 Escapes & special characters h1@

A backslash keeps a literal character where Wit would otherwise read syntax —
a colon that should not lift a parameter, a brace that should not open a record,
a comma inside a value. And quoting is the companion tool: it is how you carry
spaces, commas, and newlines through a value untouched. The recognised set
depends on context, so this page is organised by where you are writing.

@h2 Why escapes exist h2@

Wit reads certain punctuation as structure — @code : code@ lifts a
colon-scatter parameter, @@@code { code@@@ opens a record, @code , code@ separates
fields. When you want the character itself, escape it; when a value carries
whitespace, quote it.

@h2 The escape set by context h2@

@ul
@li @strong Prose text strong@ recognises @@@code \@ code@@@, @@@code \# code@@@,
@@@code \* code@@@, @@@code \_ code@@@, @@@code \~ code@@@, and @@@code \| code@@@
— the six characters that lead a structural reader (a node or definition opener,
an emphasis mark, a line comment, a parameter pipe). The backslash is dropped and
the character stays literal, so @@@code \@handle code@@@ renders a plain
@@@code @handle code@@@ and @@@code \*star\* code@@@ keeps its asterisks. Any
other character is already literal in prose, so it needs no escape. li@
@li @strong Pipe values strong@ recognise @@@code \| code@@@, which carries a
literal pipe through the value (@@@code |href a\|b| code@@@ yields
@@@code a|b code@@@). li@
@li @strong Colon-scatter strong@ uses @@@code \: code@@@ as the opt-out: it
suppresses the lift and the backslash is stripped from the output
(fixture 24-colon-scatter/body-scatter-escape). li@
@li @strong Quoted strings strong@ (in records and parameters) recognise only
@@@code \" code@@@ and @@@code \\ code@@@. li@
@li @strong Form-fill values strong@ carry text through as-is. The inline parser
does not run on them, so emphasis markers like @@@code _ code@@@ and
@@@code * code@@@ are already literal — no escape needed or consumed. li@
ul@

@h2 Quoting is the whitespace tool h2@

For a value that contains spaces, commas, or newlines, prefer a quoted string
over escaping each character:

@@@pre
#cite: { author: "Boud, D.", year: 2024 }
@thing mode:"complex value" thing@
pre@@@

The quotes protect the comma and the space and force a string type (fixtures
09-records/quoted-string-value, 24-colon-scatter/body-scatter-quoted).

@h2 The delimiter hole in parens h2@

There is one honest gap: @strong parens have no escape for their own
delimiters strong@. A literal @@@code , code@@@ or @@@code ) code@@@ inside a
parens value cannot be escaped away. Pipes do not share this gap — a pipe value
escapes its own delimiter with @@@code \| code@@@ — so when you need a comma or a
close-paren in a value, switch to a pipe, or to a body form (form-fill or
record-arg) that supports quoting.

@h2 The colon-scatter sharp edge h2@

Because the scatter contract is strict, a stray @code id:value code@ in prose
lifts a parameter you did not intend:

@@@pre
@thing the variable name:Tauraj is bad thing@
pre@@@

@code name code@ is lifted (fixture 24-colon-scatter/false-positive-prose). Fix
it with the escape — @code name\:Tauraj code@ — or a space —
@code name: Tauraj code@.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Reaching for @@@code \_ code@@@ or @@@code \* code@@@ in a form-fill value —
the marker is already literal there, so the backslash just adds a stray
character. (In prose those escapes are real and do strip.) li@
@li Expecting a paren delimiter to be escapable — rephrase, switch to a pipe, or
use a body form. li@
@li Forgetting that quotes force a string type. li@
ul@

